    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d479">

                <pb facs="0671" n="591" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg591"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 Italy/282: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-02T19:00:00+01:00">March 2, 1936—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 2—2:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>66. Your No. 17, February 27, noon. Suvich<anchor n="45" xml:id="d479-f1"/>
                    expresses real interest in the subject. He is uninformed as to financial ability
                    but will discuss with the Minister of Finance, the Foreign Exchange Control and
                    Mussolini and will talk to me in a few days. He says they have no understanding
                    with England or France and it is preferable that those Governments be not
                    advised that the matter is even being considered.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d479-f1" n="45" xml:id="d479-f1-content">
                <p>Fulvio Suvich, Italian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
